WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.199
In this lesson we're looking at escalation trigger design. Agents that

2
00:00:04.199 --> 00:00:08.640
try to handle everything on their own eventually produce bad output silently

3
00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:13.600
or get stuck in retry loops. We'll cover the trigger categories that hold up in

4
00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:17.799
production, the difference between hard and soft triggers, and how to give an

5
00:00:17.799 --> 00:00:23.559
agent a clean way to escalate deliberately. An instruction like

6
00:00:23.559 --> 00:00:27.360
escalate when you're unsure sounds reasonable and is close to useless

7
00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:32.000
because a model can't act on it consistently. What works instead is an

8
00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:36.080
escalation trigger, an explicit condition that causes the agent to hand off

9
00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:41.959
control rather than keep executing. The key word there is explicit. A confidence

10
00:00:41.959 --> 00:00:46.799
gate is one such condition. Firing when the agent's own confidence score falls

11
00:00:46.799 --> 00:00:52.080
below a threshold you define in the system prompt. Out-of-policy detection is

12
00:00:52.119 --> 00:00:57.720
another. Firing when the requested action hits a deny list or falls outside an

13
00:00:57.720 --> 00:01:02.360
allow list. Together they cover most of what you need to build a trigger set

14
00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:06.720
that actually holds up in production because each one names a condition the

15
00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:12.440
model can check rather than a feeling it has to interpret. Every escalation system

16
00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:17.720
fails in one of two directions. The first failure mode is never escalating. The

17
00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:22.839
agent keeps going, produces output, and the output reaches users even when it's

18
00:01:22.839 --> 00:01:27.400
wrong. This is the silent failure. It's the harder one to catch because there's

19
00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:34.120
no alert, no flag, just a bad result that a user may or may not notice. The second

20
00:01:34.120 --> 00:01:38.720
failure mode is escalating too often. When every ambiguous step triggers a

21
00:01:38.720 --> 00:01:43.800
handoff, reviewers get overwhelmed and start ignoring alerts. The alert channel

22
00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:49.160
loses value. Good trigger design sits between these two extremes. You want the

23
00:01:49.160 --> 00:01:54.160
agent to escalate exactly when it genuinely can't proceed safely and not

24
00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:57.720
before. Hard triggers and soft triggers work

25
00:01:57.720 --> 00:02:02.120
differently and the difference shapes how you write your system prompt. A hard

26
00:02:02.120 --> 00:02:07.080
trigger is a policy condition with no model judgment involved. If the requested

27
00:02:07.080 --> 00:02:12.399
action hits a deny list, the agent escalates. Full stop. An allow list is the

28
00:02:12.399 --> 00:02:17.199
same idea in reverse, permitting only the actions on the list, so anything else

29
00:02:17.199 --> 00:02:22.839
escalates. Both lists live in the system prompt, so the agent looks them up rather

30
00:02:22.839 --> 00:02:27.960
than reasoning about them. That predictability is the whole point. A soft

31
00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:32.839
trigger is different. It invites model judgment. If the agent's confidence is

32
00:02:32.839 --> 00:02:37.639
below a threshold or if the user's intent is ambiguous, the agent decides

33
00:02:37.639 --> 00:02:41.800
whether to escalate based on its read of the situation. You can tune that

34
00:02:41.800 --> 00:02:46.679
threshold over time as you observe how the model handles edge cases in your

35
00:02:46.679 --> 00:02:52.279
workflow. Confidence gates are one of the most useful trigger patterns. The idea is

36
00:02:52.279 --> 00:02:56.839
straightforward. The agent attaches a confidence estimate to each step and if

37
00:02:56.839 --> 00:03:01.199
that estimate drops below a threshold you've set in the system prompt, it

38
00:03:01.199 --> 00:03:05.800
escalates instead of proceeding. The threshold is something like escalate if

39
00:03:05.800 --> 00:03:11.639
confidence is below 0.7. That number lives in the policy, not in the model's

40
00:03:11.639 --> 00:03:17.399
judgment. The tricky part is calibration. A confidence number is only useful if it

41
00:03:17.399 --> 00:03:22.320
reflects reality. An overconfident model will report high confidence even when

42
00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:26.639
it's guessing, so confidence gates work best when you pair them with logged

43
00:03:26.639 --> 00:03:32.119
reasoning. The agent explains why its confidence is low and that explanation

44
00:03:32.119 --> 00:03:37.039
becomes part of the escalation record. Repeated failure detection is one of the

45
00:03:37.039 --> 00:03:40.639
simpler trigger patterns to implement and it catches a failure mode that's

46
00:03:40.639 --> 00:03:45.279
easy to overlook. An agent stuck in a retry loop is burning context window,

47
00:03:45.279 --> 00:03:50.399
wasting latency, and likely heading toward a timeout. The fix is to count.

48
00:03:50.399 --> 00:03:55.199
Count tool errors, count validation, rejections. When that count exceeds a

49
00:03:55.199 --> 00:03:59.160
ceiling you've defined, the agent escalates with a record of what it tried

50
00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:04.720
and what failed each time. The ceiling is usually two or three attempts. The exact

51
00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:09.039
number depends on your use case, but the principle is consistent. A second failure

52
00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:14.639
is information. It tells you the problem probably isn't transient. Treating it as

53
00:04:14.639 --> 00:04:20.720
an escalation trigger turns a stuck loop into a clean handoff. Policy checks and

54
00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:26.320
retry ceilings fire on their own, without the model deciding anything. The ask for

55
00:04:26.320 --> 00:04:30.480
help tool is the opposite and it's worth singling out because it's the most

56
00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:35.140
explicit form of escalation trigger available. Instead of hoping the model

57
00:04:35.220 --> 00:04:40.059
will recognize an edge case and handle it gracefully, you define a tool in the

58
00:04:40.059 --> 00:04:45.459
system prompt called something like escalate or request review. The model can

59
00:04:45.459 --> 00:04:50.700
call it just like any other tool it has access to. When it does, it logs the

60
00:04:50.700 --> 00:04:55.980
decision, the context, and the reason all in one step. That's the advantage over

61
00:04:55.980 --> 00:05:01.660
softer signals. A called function produces a clean structured record. Compare that

62
00:05:01.660 --> 00:05:05.920
to an agent that writes a free text message explaining why it stopped, which

63
00:05:05.920 --> 00:05:11.899
is harder to route, harder to parse, and easier to miss. It's worth being just as

64
00:05:11.899 --> 00:05:16.660
precise about the triggers that should never fire because over escalation is

65
00:05:16.660 --> 00:05:21.179
the quieter of the two failure modes. Escalating on every uncertain step

66
00:05:21.179 --> 00:05:26.220
defeats the point of automating the work at all. If an action is low stakes and

67
00:05:26.459 --> 00:05:30.859
easily reversed, a human gate adds friction and buys you no safety in

68
00:05:30.859 --> 00:05:35.299
return. If a validation layer already checks something programmatically, putting

69
00:05:35.299 --> 00:05:39.779
a person behind it as well is redundant and it teaches reviewers that approvals

70
00:05:39.779 --> 00:05:44.579
are a formality. And a trigger based on gut feel rather than a defined condition

71
00:05:44.579 --> 00:05:49.179
fires inconsistently, which means you can't test it or reason about when it

72
00:05:49.179 --> 00:05:54.739
will happen. Each of these looks cautious and is actually corrosive. Here's the

73
00:05:54.739 --> 00:05:58.940
framing that keeps the whole thing honest. Human review is a targeted risk

74
00:05:58.940 --> 00:06:04.059
control placed deliberately where consequences are high and hard to

75
00:06:04.059 --> 00:06:08.980
reverse. It isn't a catch-all for whenever the model seems unsure. Getting

76
00:06:08.980 --> 00:06:13.019
the threshold right is a calibration exercise and it isn't a decision you

77
00:06:13.019 --> 00:06:18.299
make once. Set thresholds too tight and you get constant interruption, reviewers

78
00:06:18.299 --> 00:06:23.260
stop reading and the automation stops earning its keep. Set them too loose and

79
00:06:23.260 --> 00:06:28.179
real risk walks straight through the gate unchecked. So watch your escalation

80
00:06:28.179 --> 00:06:32.700
rates and production and tune them against what reviewers actually report.

81
00:06:32.700 --> 00:06:37.940
The threshold that is right today can be wrong once the workflow around it

82
00:06:37.940 --> 00:06:43.500
changes. A trigger is the moment an agent admits it shouldn't be the one deciding.

83
00:06:43.500 --> 00:06:48.739
Getting that moment right is the whole game because you can miss it in both

84
00:06:48.739 --> 00:06:54.220
directions. Silence ships bad output nobody reviewed and noise trains your

85
00:06:54.220 --> 00:06:59.459
reviewers to approve on reflex. Just remember a trigger that fires on

86
00:06:59.459 --> 00:07:04.540
something reversible costs attention and buys you nothing. That wraps up this one.

87
00:07:04.540 --> 00:07:08.260
I'll see you in the next lesson.

